## Goal
Run a sample PCA from the gene counts and produce one plot of the samples on the first two
principal components, coloured by treated versus untreated (the design's `condition` column),
so the user can see how the samples group by treatment.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| `counts_gene` (native) | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper` | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv` |
| `design` (native) | `01_prepare_samplesheets` | `01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv` |

## Method
All steps run in one Python script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, in the `gars-bio` conda
environment (pandas 3.0.5, numpy 2.5.2, pydeseq2 0.5.4, scikit-learn 1.9.0, matplotlib
3.11.1 — all in `_references/gars-bio.lock.txt`; nothing is installed). Inputs are read only;
nothing is written outside `results/`.

1. Resolve both inputs at run time with
   `python3 _system/resolve_artifact.py --project projects/run-dc266b24 --assay rnaseq_bulk --consumes counts_gene design`;
   stop if resolution fails.
2. Read the count matrix; set `gene_id` as the index and drop `gene_name`. Read the design
   table. Order the count columns by the design's `sample_id`s; stop if the two sample sets
   differ (expected: A1–A3 untreated, B1–B3 treated).
3. Round the length-scaled counts to integers (pydeseq2 requires integer counts; this is the
   standard handling of tximport length-scaled counts). Drop genes with zero counts in every
   sample.
4. Variance-stabilising transform: `pydeseq2.dds.DeseqDataSet(counts=<samples x genes>,
   metadata=<design>, design="~condition")`, then `dds.vst(use_design=False)` — i.e. blind to
   the condition, as for exploratory QC — taking `dds.layers["vst_counts"]`.
5. Select the 500 genes with the highest variance across samples of the VST values (the
   DESeq2 `plotPCA` default, `ntop=500`).
6. PCA with `sklearn.decomposition.PCA` on those 500 genes, centred, not scaled
   (`n_components` = number of samples, i.e. 6). Record each sample's scores and each
   component's explained-variance ratio.
7. Plot PC1 vs PC2 with matplotlib: one point per sample, coloured by `condition`
   (treated / untreated, with a legend), each point labelled with its `sample_id`, axis
   labels giving the percent variance explained (e.g. `PC1 (xx.x%)`). Save as PNG at 300 dpi.
8. Write the scores and variance tables listed under Outputs.

